    "result_text": "There's only one test class, and it passes. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec asked for an immutable structured audit event `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event:'OWNER_CREATED'}` emitted via the `AUDIT` logger alongside the existing audit line, with `seq` monotonically increasing across creates and the event carrying the owner's current primary identifier (customerCode now, memberId later).\n\n**To keep structural impact low**, I avoided growing the large `OwnerRestControllerV1` class or its methods:\n\n1. **New file** `OwnerCreatedEvent.java` \u2014 a small, cohesive immutable `record` (WMC_other near zero) that owns all the new behaviour: the monotonic `seq` counter, JSON rendering with stable field order, and the \"primary identifier\" abstraction. The `next(ownerId, primaryIdentifier, membershipLevel)` factory takes the identifier as a parameter, so when the customerCode is later unified into a memberId, only the *caller* changes \u2014 this class stays untouched.\n\n2. **One added line** in `addOwner`:\n   ```java\n   AUDIT.info(OwnerCreatedEvent.next(owner.getId(), owner.getCustomerCode(), ownerMapper.membershipLevel(owner)).toJson());\n   ```\n   `addOwner`'s cyclomatic complexity stays 1 and only a single line changed, minimizing the `WMC_other \u00d7 CC \u00d7 changed_lines` cost that the big controller class would otherwise multiply.\n\nTwo files touched, one changed line in a complex class, all new complexity isolated in a trivial new unit. The target test and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`), with the event logging as:\n```\n{\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":2,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}\n```",
